 159: ## 18. 2026-11-09: diagnostic before any redesign: KC threshold and sparseness
 160: 
 161: - **Why this diagnostic is allowed:** it answers one named, gate-relevant question that blocks the feedback experiment (can the gamma-KC code separate moves on one board at a sourced sparseness?). Read-only, dev boards (dry-run packet), no learning, 3 boards x 6 thresholds. Next feedback-enabled step: a revision-3 proposal with the chosen threshold, reviewed, then a new dry run.
 162: - **Root-cause finding (code, not hypothesis):** `trainer.mjs:44` sets the bias (constant drive) of **every** neuron to 0.01. So all 134,209 model neurons are tonically driven, and every KC, DAN and MBON fires whatever the board is. The board's signal is a small ripple on that drive. This explains the dense, uniform KC rates (0.0165 +/- 0.0015), the endogenous D of about -0.009 without a pulse, and MBON05 at about 0.023. Revision 2 did not mention this default; neither did I. The diagnostic script was built by Haiku; I changed its threshold to be relative to that default (KC bias = 0.01 - theta, so theta 0 reproduces the dry run).
 163: - **Sources (verified from abstracts via Europe PMC):** Turner et al. 2008, J Neurophysiol, PMID 18094099: "KC firing thresholds are high"; KC responses "highly selective and, thus sparse"; representations become less correlated. Lin et al. 2014, Nat Neurosci, PMID 24561998: APL feedback maintains "sparse, decorrelated odor coding"; disrupting it "prevented flies from learning to discriminate similar, but not dissimilar, odors". Honegger et al. 2011, J Neurosci, PMID 21849538: robust sparse coding. **Not verified:** the "5-10% of KCs respond to a given odor" figure (search summary of Lin et al.'s introduction; full text was blocked or unreadable: PMC challenge page, journal 403, author PDF refused, repository PDF not text-extractable).
 164: - **Source limit for the design:** the sources support high KC thresholds and sparse codes in kind, for odour input. None gives a threshold value for this rate model or for symbolic chess input. Any threshold must be chosen by a predeclared sparseness target before data, not by chess outcomes.
