 225: ## 26. 2026-11-09: widened-mask feasibility screen (question recorded before running)
 226: 
 227: Sources for widening: Gkanias et al. 2022 Table 1 (verbatim via eLife page: 12 neurons, 8 DAN-to-MBON plastic connections, roles sat/sav/rat/rav/mat/mav, dat/dav/cat/cav/fat/fav; PAM-gamma4<gamma1gamma2 named reward-encoding verbatim). Connectome types from the pinned FBbt ontology synonyms: MBON11 (gamma1pedc), MBON05 (gamma4>gamma1gamma2), MBON12 (gamma2alpha'1), MBON01 (gamma5beta'2a), MBON02 (beta2beta'2a), MBON15 (alpha'1; MBON31 also matched a prefix, to resolve), PAM07, PAM02 (beta'2a), PAM03 (beta2beta'2a), PPL101 (gamma1pedc), PPL103 (gamma2alpha'1). **Not verified verbatim:** PAM-beta'2a as reward-encoding (from a page summary). Screen (`python actions/incentive_mask_screen_v1.py`, mine): with gains in [0.2, 1.8] on every KC input to the six MBON types and the source valence readout (attraction minus avoidance), can a safe capture be made top? KC-drive proxy only (no dynamics, non-KC inputs ignored): a necessary-capacity screen, not an exact test.
 228: - **Result** (`runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/incentive-mask-screen-v1.log`): 6,046 mask edges onto 16 MBON cells (MBON11 2, MBON12 4, MBON02 2, MBON05 2, MBON01 2, MBON15 4). Best safe-capture margin relative to the board's largest proxy score: default drive +2.6e-3, +6.4e-4, +3.1e-5; zero drive +1.0e-4, +1.3e-5, +4.7e-5. Positive on all 3 boards at both drives. Reading: the widened, sourced mask passes the capacity screen that the gamma4 mask fails (item 24), and at the trainer's default drive, so no unsourced drive or scale calibration is needed. Limits: proxy (no dynamics, non-KC inputs ignored; at default drive the gamma4 linear model was wrong in item 24), 3 boards, margins small. Script fix on the way: first run used wrong npz keys (my mistake; the file's CSR arrays are `csr_indptr`/`csr_indices`/`syn_count`).
 229: - **Pivot:** design revision 4 = the full Gkanias incentive circuit (8 plastic DAN-to-MBON connections, valence readout), keeping Eyes v2, default drive and the revision-2 pipeline. Proposal next, then strict review before any build.
   1: # Fly connections memory v1: stop report (2026-11-09)
   2: 
   3: **P1-P3 unmet. Stopped under the goal's stop clause: a required fact cannot be resolved from sources.** Volume 2, chapters 3 and 5. No full run was launched; the sealed test games (development bucket) remain unused.
   4: 
   5: ## What does not hold (limits first)
   6: 
   7: 1. The published gamma4 rule (Gkanias et al. 2022) never changed a choice in this model. In the dry run, 0/8 A and 0/8 B boards were acquired, and test success equalled learning-off (1/20). Development data only.
   8: 2. With the trainer's default drive (bias 0.01 in all 134,209 neurons, `trainer.mjs:44`), every gamma KC fires tonically. Two moves on one board share about 97% of their KC pattern, so learning shifts all moves together.
   9: 3. With zero drive, the code becomes sparse (14% of gamma KCs active, which sources describe as sparse), but KC rates fall to about 0.0005-0.0009, roughly 1,000 times below saturation. The rule's change is proportional to KC rate, so 16 events moved the practised move's score by 1.7e-7, and its rank did not change.
  10: 4. A 10-100 times stronger eye stimulus barely raises KC rates (0.0009), because early visual stages saturate.
  11: 5. Even gains chosen by an oracle at the rule's bounds (0.2 or 1.8), set to favour the safe capture over its top rival, never made a safe capture the top choice on 3 development boards; a second rival always overtook. Greedy construction: strong evidence, not a proof.
  12: 6. Not shown: any biological limit of the living fly, or a proof for the raw connectome model. These are finite measurements on 2-3 development boards.
  13: 
  14: ## What was tried
  15: 
  16: | Step | Result | Evidence |
  17: |---|---|---|
  18: | Revision 2 build (packet, condition, judge, auditor, mapping check) | 135/135 tests; Codex code review r1 required changes, all fixed | journal items 11-14 |
  19: | Dry run, 6 arms, seed 7300 | complete, "does not pass"; audit 9/10 PASS (pins FAIL from my post-registration edits only) | items 16, 19 |
  20: | Codex diagnosis | dense-KC hypothesis supported; STOP FOR RESEARCHER DECISION | [diagnosis](diagnosis-r1.md) |
  21: | KC threshold sweep | KCs stay active (network drive); all-or-none switch | item 18 |
  22: | Zero drive | sparse (14%), 20 times more move-specific, but rates about 0.0005 | item 18 |
  23: | Learning probe at zero drive | no ranking change after 16 events | item 18 |
  24: | Eye-gain sweep | KC rates barely rise | item 18 |
  25: | Probe v2: KC term on the model's own scale | scores move 18%, but all moves move together; margin levels off below 0 | item 21 |
  26: | Oracle gains at the rule's bounds (greedy, 3 boards, both drives) | never makes a safe capture top; cycles between rivals; closest margin -1.5e-8 | item 22 |
  27: | Multi-rival oracle bound (dual of max-min, exact verification) | zero drive: no gain setting works on 2 of 3 boards (linear bound about -2e-6, model error 2-15%); default drive: linear model invalid, exact oracle settings lose | item 24 |
  28: 
  29: ## The unresolved fact
  30: 
  31: The rule is written for normalized activity (0-1). The model's KC rates are in different units, and the tonic drive level of each neuron type is an engineering default. To make the rule act, one must choose either a per-neuron baseline drive or a KC activity scale for the rule. No source gives either value for this rate model. Choosing it from chess outcomes would be tuning; choosing it from activity alone (for example, KC scale = maximum KC rate on development boards) is an unsourced engineering calibration that a strict reviewer would have to accept as such.
  32: 
  33: ## Best evidence now
  34: 
  35: - **Volume 2:** development evidence that teaching reaches identified PAM07 neurons and changes only the fly's KC-to-MBON05 synapses (P1 mechanics, audited), but no learning of safe-capture choice.
  36: - **Volume 4 (strongest result):** at zero drive, a linear certificate, checked against the exact network, shows that on 2 of 3 development boards no setting of the 2,096 KC-to-MBON05 gains within the rule's bounds makes any safe capture the top choice, so no rule confined to these synapses can pass acquisition there. Also a measured, not proven, limit of the raw connectome rate model: the eye-to-KC path either drowns the board signal in tonic drive or attenuates it about 1,000-fold. A proof needs a bound on the change in pairwise move-score differences over the declared domain.
  37: 
  38: ## The one decision needed
  39: 
  40: The oracle result makes the calibration route unlikely to pass even if authorized: the limit appears to lie in these synapses' reach over moves that share most of their KCs, not only in the rule's units. Do you authorize **one disclosed engineering calibration** (zero tonic drive plus a KC activity scale for the rule, fixed from development-board activity only, before any data), as revision 3 with new review and a new dry run? If not, the alternatives are to record this as the Volume 2 negative and Volume 4 measurement, or to run the full test as registered (about 96 CPU-hours) for a confirmed negative.
   1: ## 1. What the dry run shows
   2: 
   3: **The dense-KC hypothesis is supported as a description of this modelâ€™s recorded activity, but not yet proved as the cause of the behavioral failure.** The trace reports every mapped KC active, weak differences between practised cues, near-equal legal-move scores, and a nearly common score shift after learning ([journal, lines 141â€“144](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:141>)). Those observations fit the hypothesis that the activity-gated update writes broadly without separating moves. Under the implemented rule, when gain starts at 1, the first change is proportional to DAN factor times KC activity ([rule, lines 78â€“96](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/reversal-memory.mjs:78>)).
   4: 
   5: Ranked explanations:
   6: 
   7: 1. **Weak within-board KC separation plus broadly positive activity.** This is the strongest explanation: the recorded cue patterns differ a little, but all KCs contribute positive activity to the update. The observed 1,809 mask edges with at least 1% cumulative change show that weights move; the absence of B-phase pressure writes on A-written edges and zero A/B acquisition show that broad change did not produce the desired behavior ([journal](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:141>)).
   8: 2. **The MBON05 score may have little move-specific sensitivity to those gains.** Scores are the negative mean of two MBON05 rates; other network inputs can contribute to those rates, and the code does not decompose KC-driven from non-KC-driven output ([condition, lines 235â€“245](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:235>)). Non-KC dominance is plausible, not established.
   9: 3. **APL and encoding may leave the KC population too tonic.** The local APL adapter replaces KC currents with a spatially filtered APL field, but has no explicit KC active-set threshold ([APL, lines 75â€“89](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-apl-v1.mjs:75>)). The tests show the adapter follows its reference implementation; they do not show that its production field produces biologically sparse chess responses.
  10: 4. **Eight steps and the small teaching budget may limit acquisition.** They are possible contributors, but the full run keeps the same eight-step forward pass. More boards alone will not fix an uninformative representation or readout.
  11: 5. **Tie tolerance is not the explanation.** The ranking tie tolerance is about \(3\times10^{-9}\) at scores near âˆ’0.023 ([metrics, lines 5â€“8 and 45â€“47](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:5>)). The reported score spread is tens of thousands of times larger. Scores are close, but they are not being collapsed into ties by the tolerance.
  12: 
  13: The input is not wholly absent from the mapped KCs: the recorded patterns differ across cues. That does **not** establish a biologically faithful visual route. Project scope says the symbolic chess encoders bypass modeled eyes; natural vision is unimplemented.
  14: 
  15: ## 2. Protocol and gates
  16: 
  17: **The core arm mechanics and exposure controls look right.** The primary sends its external current to the mapped DAN indices, extracts actual endpoint KC and DAN rates, and updates only when the arm is configured to learn ([condition, lines 164â€“176](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:164>); [teaching, lines 94â€“120](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-teaching-v1.mjs:94>)). The score path computes a fixed MBON05 score and applies outcomes only afterward for metrics ([condition, lines 205â€“245](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:205>)). The absent arm still runs endogenous updates; off skips them. The requested tests verify these distinctions.
  18: 
  19: Arms use the same tape and rank at every checkpoint; shuffled permutes pulse booleans within each phase while preserving counts ([job, lines 73â€“101 and 104â€“121](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:73>); [tape builder, lines 268â€“280](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-packet-v1.mjs:268>)). The off-arm reuse cut is implemented: the job writes no reused rankings. The file-format document still describes `ranking-reused` as allowed, so reconcile that stale contract with the code before confirmation.
  20: 
  21: Every legal move is scored; `scoreAllLegal` rejects a ranking that omits any move ([condition, lines 205â€“232](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:205>); [metrics, lines 18â€“43](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:18>)). Game groups are assigned by a hash of game ID, independently of neural scores, and the packet builder rejects repeated games, board-key collisions, and rendered-input collisions ([config builder, lines 2â€“17](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/inputs/make_packet_configs.py:2>); [packet preparation, lines 112â€“137](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/prepare-fly-connections-packet-v1.mjs:112>)). Test games are separate from practice games. The clues are broadly honest, but the opening list is not a complete stand-alone scope statement: it should also foreground the one-ply capture criterion and the task-selected test set; the latter is disclosed later in gates section 4b.3.
  22: 
  23: **There is one red criterion in the teaching-causality gate.** The judge compares informative and controls on pooled A+B success, while its own-baseline check is split by group ([judge, lines 218â€“237](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-judge-v1.mjs:218>)). A strong effect in A could conceal a failure to beat controls in B, or vice versa. That can pass while group-specific teaching dependence fails. Revision 2 requires the A/B learning claim; make the control comparisons separate for A and B in the gates, judge, auditor, and counterexample tests before confirmation. The current dry run fails regardless, so this does not change its reported verdict.
  24: 
  25: The other main gates match the stated margins: A retention at both post-B and final, B acquisition against its own post-R1 baseline, effective-write pressure, R correction, and new-game comparisons against off and shuffled ([gates, lines 35â€“53](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-gates-2026-11-08.md:35>); [retention judge, lines 85â€“123](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:85>)).
  26: 
  27: ## 3. Source-supported redesign options
  28: 
  29: - **Calibrate existing KC/APL dynamics:** Honegger et al. report sparse odor-evoked KC responses, and Lin et al. show APL feedback helps sparsify and decorrelate odor responses ([Honegger et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3180869/); [Lin et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4000970/)). This supports sparse coding as a biological design target, **not** a 5â€“10% target for this symbolic chess input or visual gamma-d KCs. A fixed, predeclared adjustment to modeled APL/KC dynamics could leave learning confined to the registered mask, but an outcome-tuned threshold or top-k filter would be an unsupported extra computation and would need a new registration.
  30: - **Subtract a resting activity vector:** this is not directly supported by the cited rule. In Gkanias et al., \(w_\text{rest}=1\) is the **resting synaptic weight**, not a resting firing-rate baseline ([Gkanias et al., equations 18â€“20](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611)). `ReversalMemory.observe(rest)` subtracts a neuronal-rate vector; revision 2 expressly uses zero and rejects counterfactual cue/DAN subtraction ([revision 2, lines 7â€“15](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-revision-2-2026-11-07.md:7>)). A blank-trial baseline would therefore change the registered rule and should not be slipped in as a parameter tweak.
  31: - **Restrict to visual gamma-d KCs:** Vogt et al. support a visual role for gamma-d KCs and visual responses in some of them ([Vogt et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4884080/)). Anatomical restriction is not extra-circuit arithmetic if fixed before training, but it only helps if the current encoder actually drives this visual pathway and the selected KCs have the relevant MBON05/PAM07 connections. It narrows the claim; it does not itself fix sparse coding or score sensitivity.
  32: 
  33: None of these sources validates a specific numerical correction for the present model. Choose and justify a mechanism before a new confirmatory design; do not tune against this dry-run outcome and then call the same run confirmatory.
  34: 
  35: ## 4. Audit, resources, scope, and limits
  36: 
  37: The auditor recomputes score metrics, update arithmetic, gates, and bootstrap without importing producer metrics, judge, or job modules ([audit module, lines 1â€“19](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-audit-v1.mjs:1>)). It does reuse packet code for tape construction and certificate verification, so it is not fully independent of packet semantics. Its graph replay is a useful reproducibility sampleâ€”four-event phase prefixes extended to both pulse kinds, plus two test-board rankingsâ€”not a replay of every teaching event ([audit runner, lines 40â€“43 and 52â€“99](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/audit-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:40>)). Static audit recomputes every receiptâ€™s update from recorded rates, but only sampled events establish those rates against a fresh graph pass. At inspection, there was no `audit.json`, so the dry-run audit verdict remains unknown. The requested unit tests finished **132/132 passing**; that validates the tested fixtures, not the missing production audit.
  38: 
  39: Resume truncates unacknowledged JSONL and restores the acknowledged snapshot; the suite includes a passing exactly-once interruption test. Writes use temporary files plus rename, worker and runner guards check RSS/free memory, and worker failure stops new jobs without silent retry ([job, lines 52â€“65](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:52>); [runner, lines 70â€“104](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/run-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:70>)). The file contract requires transitive pins, but the pin checker validates only hashes that are listed; it does not prove the list is complete ([formats, line 24](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-file-formats-2026-11-08.md:24>); [pin check, lines 865â€“880](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-audit-v1.mjs:865>)).
  40: 
  41: The declared KC-key-store and second-branch geometry cuts are acceptable if claims stay limited to the registered neural comparison and midpoint geometry. The off-arm ranking reuse was correctly removed. **Another planned cut was not implemented:** B-end still ranks testA/testB in both job code and file contract ([job, lines 11â€“16](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:11>); [formats, lines 51â€“61](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-file-formats-2026-11-08.md:51>)). Recompute the full-run pass and wall budget with those rankings, or remove them consistently before pinning. The runner also explicitly requires `--cpu-duty 1`; treat that as full-utilization execution, not a pacing control ([runner, lines 22â€“24](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/run-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:22>)).
  42: 
  43: The dry run is useful Volume 2 development evidence that this implementation engages plasticity but did not acquire or transfer the criterion on its small sample. It is not confirmatory evidence. It does not prove a Volume 4 limit. A limit claim needs a bound over the declared state/input/training domain on the **change in pairwise score differences**, e.g.  
  44: \(\sup_{t,i,j}|(s_i-s_j)_t-(s_i-s_j)_0|\),  
  45: and a comparison to the smallest initial ranking margin. The observed nearly common shift on these boards is only a finite empirical measurement, not such a bound.
  46: 
  47: **STOP FOR RESEARCHER DECISION (choose between a new, source-bounded visual-KC/readout redesign and spending the full-run budget on the negative result for this exact model).**

