 189: ## 23. 2026-11-09: remaining routes all need the researcher
 190: 
 191: After the hook's second ruling I checked the last route not yet examined: input design. Every candidate is shown as a whole-board picture through Eyes v2, so moves on one board look about 97% alike. The repository's move-centred encoders (`public-action-relative-cue*.mjs`, `public-attack-map-cue*.mjs`) separate moves well (same-board overlap 0.18 against 0.86, per their README) but drive KCs directly through hashed tokens, bypassing vision; the restart README marks them frozen and "do not use them for new work", and the attack-map cue computes an attack judgement for the fly (a clue). Conclusion: each remaining path to P1-P3 changes a commitment that only the researcher can change: (1) the input pathway (Eyes v2, restart decision 2026-11-03), (2) the rule's activity units, (3) the neurons' baseline drive. None is resolvable from sources for this model. No code changed in this step.
 192: 
 193: ## 24. 2026-11-09: multi-rival oracle bound (question recorded before running)
 194: 
 195: After the hook's third ruling: the decisive remaining question is whether ANY setting of the 2,096 mask gains in [0.2, 1.8] makes a safe capture the top choice over all legal moves. Method: dump each legal move's per-edge presynaptic KC rates (`probe-oracle-lp-v1.mjs`, Haiku-built), fit a linear score model (sensitivity from an all-gains-0.9 ranking), solve max-min over all rivals through its dual (`python actions/solve_oracle_lp_v1.py`, mine; self-checked: 0.6 on a solvable case, -1.0 certified on an impossible one), then verify the best gains exactly on the graph. A positive exact margin is an existence witness (the rule, not the synapses, is the limit). A negative dual bound is a certificate **for the linear model only**; its error is measured by comparing predicted and exact margins. Development boards Q04sIPQ5:13, SjmcbcGu:16, 5XRGyGSj:13; bias 0 and 0.01.
 196: - **Result** (`runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/oracle-lp-v1/`, logs `oracle-lp-v1.log` and `-verify.log`). First verify attempt failed: 1 - 0.8 rounds to 0.19999999999999996 < 0.2; fixed by clipping in the solver (my mistake), verification rerun.
 197: 
 198: | Drive | Board | Linear dual upper bound (best safe) | Best setting found, predicted | Exact margin of that setting |
 199: |---|---|---|---|---|
 200: | 0 | Q04sIPQ5:13 | -2.06e-6 (f6e4) | -2.07e-6 | -2.39e-6 |
 201: | 0 | SjmcbcGu:16 | -2.20e-6 (f2e3) | -2.22e-6 | -2.26e-6 |
 202: | 0 | 5XRGyGSj:13 | +1.39e-7 (g8g7) | -1.42e-8 | -1.46e-8 |
 203: | 0.01 | Q04sIPQ5:13 | +4.89e-7 | +3.33e-7 | -4.47e-6 |
 204: | 0.01 | SjmcbcGu:16 | +6.36e-7 | +4.71e-7 | -5.98e-6 |
 205: | 0.01 | 5XRGyGSj:13 | +4.30e-7 | +3.61e-7 | -6.00e-6 |
 206: 
 207: Reading: at zero drive the linear model predicts the exact margins within 2-15%, and its dual bound says no gain setting in [0.2, 1.8] makes any safe capture top on 2 of 3 boards (bounds about -2e-6, ten times the largest observed prediction error, 3.2e-7). The third board is unresolved (dual gap). At the default drive the linear model is wrong (predicted wins, exact losses of 4.5-6e-6), so no certificate there; the oracle settings still lose. **Volume 4 candidate:** on these boards, with whole-board Eyes v2 input, the KC-to-MBON05 synapses cannot by themselves make the fly choose a safe capture, so no learning rule confined to them could pass P2 acquisition there. Limits: 3 development boards; linear certificate with measured, not bounded, error; one APL geometry; one score readout. Not a proof for the raw connectome model in general.
 208: 
 209: ## 25. 2026-11-09: KC representation capacity (question recorded before running)
 210: 
 211: After the hook's fourth ruling: before any "widen" design (more plastic compartments), test whether the KC population as a whole carries the needed information. Question: does ANY linear readout of all 5,177 KC endpoint rates make a safe capture score above every other legal move (signed weights; and positive, excitatory-only weights)? Same max-min dual solver (`python actions/kc_capacity_v1.py`). If no readout works, widening is pointless and the input is the limit; if one works, the gamma4 mask is the bottleneck and widening has a concrete target. Data: `diagnose-kc-sparseness-v1.mjs --dump-all` (new option, mine), 3 dev boards, default drive and zero drive.
 212: - **Result** (`runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/kc-capacity-v1.log`; KC rates scaled by the board's largest rate). Best safe-capture margin over every other legal move, any linear readout of all 5,177 KCs:
 213: 
 214: | Drive | Board | Signed weights: found (upper) | Positive weights: found (upper) |
 215: |---|---|---|---|
 216: | 0.01 | Q04sIPQ5:13 | +0.139 (0.161) | +0.0142 (0.0219) |
 217: | 0.01 | SjmcbcGu:16 | +0.067 (0.106) | +0.0556 (0.0587) |
 218: | 0.01 | 5XRGyGSj:13 | +0.055 (0.071) | +0.0049 (0.0075) |
 219: | 0 | Q04sIPQ5:13 | +0.148 (0.241) | +0.0373 (0.0398) |
 220: | 0 | SjmcbcGu:16 | +0.072 (0.101) | +0.0232 (0.0235) |
 221: | 0 | 5XRGyGSj:13 | -0.019 found (0.077) | +0.00007 (0.0004) |
 222: 
 223: Reading: the KC population does carry move-distinguishing information; a readout exists on every board (positive weights included; the zero-drive board 3 margin is tiny). So the limit found in item 24 is the **gamma4 mask** (gamma KCs only, one MBON type, gains bounded around fixed anatomical weights), not the KC code. Limits: with 23-46 moves in 5,177 dimensions linear separability is nearly guaranteed, so this shows acquisition capacity only, not transfer (P3); the readout is an oracle, not a sourced circuit. **Implication for design:** a "widen" revision (more KC classes and MBON compartments, each with a primary-sourced DAN pairing) is the only route found that keeps Eyes v2 and could make acquisition possible.
 224: 
 225: ## 26. 2026-11-09: widened-mask feasibility screen (question recorded before running)
 226: 
 227: Sources for widening: Gkanias et al. 2022 Table 1 (verbatim via eLife page: 12 neurons, 8 DAN-to-MBON plastic connections, roles sat/sav/rat/rav/mat/mav, dat/dav/cat/cav/fat/fav; PAM-gamma4<gamma1gamma2 named reward-encoding verbatim). Connectome types from the pinned FBbt ontology synonyms: MBON11 (gamma1pedc), MBON05 (gamma4>gamma1gamma2), MBON12 (gamma2alpha'1), MBON01 (gamma5beta'2a), MBON02 (beta2beta'2a), MBON15 (alpha'1; MBON31 also matched a prefix, to resolve), PAM07, PAM02 (beta'2a), PAM03 (beta2beta'2a), PPL101 (gamma1pedc), PPL103 (gamma2alpha'1). **Not verified verbatim:** PAM-beta'2a as reward-encoding (from a page summary). Screen (`python actions/incentive_mask_screen_v1.py`, mine): with gains in [0.2, 1.8] on every KC input to the six MBON types and the source valence readout (attraction minus avoidance), can a safe capture be made top? KC-drive proxy only (no dynamics, non-KC inputs ignored): a necessary-capacity screen, not an exact test.
 228: - **Result** (`runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/incentive-mask-screen-v1.log`): 6,046 mask edges onto 16 MBON cells (MBON11 2, MBON12 4, MBON02 2, MBON05 2, MBON01 2, MBON15 4). Best safe-capture margin relative to the board's largest proxy score: default drive +2.6e-3, +6.4e-4, +3.1e-5; zero drive +1.0e-4, +1.3e-5, +4.7e-5. Positive on all 3 boards at both drives. Reading: the widened, sourced mask passes the capacity screen that the gamma4 mask fails (item 24), and at the trainer's default drive, so no unsourced drive or scale calibration is needed. Limits: proxy (no dynamics, non-KC inputs ignored; at default drive the gamma4 linear model was wrong in item 24), 3 boards, margins small. Script fix on the way: first run used wrong npz keys (my mistake; the file's CSR arrays are `csr_indptr`/`csr_indices`/`syn_count`).
 229: - **Pivot:** design revision 4 = the full Gkanias incentive circuit (8 plastic DAN-to-MBON connections, valence readout), keeping Eyes v2, default drive and the revision-2 pipeline. Proposal next, then strict review before any build.
   1: ## 1. What the dry run shows
   2: 
   3: **The dense-KC hypothesis is supported as a description of this modelâ€™s recorded activity, but not yet proved as the cause of the behavioral failure.** The trace reports every mapped KC active, weak differences between practised cues, near-equal legal-move scores, and a nearly common score shift after learning ([journal, lines 141â€“144](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:141>)). Those observations fit the hypothesis that the activity-gated update writes broadly without separating moves. Under the implemented rule, when gain starts at 1, the first change is proportional to DAN factor times KC activity ([rule, lines 78â€“96](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/reversal-memory.mjs:78>)).
   4: 
   5: Ranked explanations:
   6: 
   7: 1. **Weak within-board KC separation plus broadly positive activity.** This is the strongest explanation: the recorded cue patterns differ a little, but all KCs contribute positive activity to the update. The observed 1,809 mask edges with at least 1% cumulative change show that weights move; the absence of B-phase pressure writes on A-written edges and zero A/B acquisition show that broad change did not produce the desired behavior ([journal](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:141>)).
   8: 2. **The MBON05 score may have little move-specific sensitivity to those gains.** Scores are the negative mean of two MBON05 rates; other network inputs can contribute to those rates, and the code does not decompose KC-driven from non-KC-driven output ([condition, lines 235â€“245](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:235>)). Non-KC dominance is plausible, not established.
   9: 3. **APL and encoding may leave the KC population too tonic.** The local APL adapter replaces KC currents with a spatially filtered APL field, but has no explicit KC active-set threshold ([APL, lines 75â€“89](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-apl-v1.mjs:75>)). The tests show the adapter follows its reference implementation; they do not show that its production field produces biologically sparse chess responses.
  10: 4. **Eight steps and the small teaching budget may limit acquisition.** They are possible contributors, but the full run keeps the same eight-step forward pass. More boards alone will not fix an uninformative representation or readout.
  11: 5. **Tie tolerance is not the explanation.** The ranking tie tolerance is about \(3\times10^{-9}\) at scores near âˆ’0.023 ([metrics, lines 5â€“8 and 45â€“47](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:5>)). The reported score spread is tens of thousands of times larger. Scores are close, but they are not being collapsed into ties by the tolerance.
  12: 
  13: The input is not wholly absent from the mapped KCs: the recorded patterns differ across cues. That does **not** establish a biologically faithful visual route. Project scope says the symbolic chess encoders bypass modeled eyes; natural vision is unimplemented.
  14: 
  15: ## 2. Protocol and gates
  16: 
  17: **The core arm mechanics and exposure controls look right.** The primary sends its external current to the mapped DAN indices, extracts actual endpoint KC and DAN rates, and updates only when the arm is configured to learn ([condition, lines 164â€“176](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:164>); [teaching, lines 94â€“120](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-teaching-v1.mjs:94>)). The score path computes a fixed MBON05 score and applies outcomes only afterward for metrics ([condition, lines 205â€“245](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:205>)). The absent arm still runs endogenous updates; off skips them. The requested tests verify these distinctions.
  18: 
  19: Arms use the same tape and rank at every checkpoint; shuffled permutes pulse booleans within each phase while preserving counts ([job, lines 73â€“101 and 104â€“121](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:73>); [tape builder, lines 268â€“280](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-packet-v1.mjs:268>)). The off-arm reuse cut is implemented: the job writes no reused rankings. The file-format document still describes `ranking-reused` as allowed, so reconcile that stale contract with the code before confirmation.
  20: 
  21: Every legal move is scored; `scoreAllLegal` rejects a ranking that omits any move ([condition, lines 205â€“232](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-condition-v1.mjs:205>); [metrics, lines 18â€“43](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:18>)). Game groups are assigned by a hash of game ID, independently of neural scores, and the packet builder rejects repeated games, board-key collisions, and rendered-input collisions ([config builder, lines 2â€“17](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/inputs/make_packet_configs.py:2>); [packet preparation, lines 112â€“137](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/prepare-fly-connections-packet-v1.mjs:112>)). Test games are separate from practice games. The clues are broadly honest, but the opening list is not a complete stand-alone scope statement: it should also foreground the one-ply capture criterion and the task-selected test set; the latter is disclosed later in gates section 4b.3.
  22: 
  23: **There is one red criterion in the teaching-causality gate.** The judge compares informative and controls on pooled A+B success, while its own-baseline check is split by group ([judge, lines 218â€“237](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-judge-v1.mjs:218>)). A strong effect in A could conceal a failure to beat controls in B, or vice versa. That can pass while group-specific teaching dependence fails. Revision 2 requires the A/B learning claim; make the control comparisons separate for A and B in the gates, judge, auditor, and counterexample tests before confirmation. The current dry run fails regardless, so this does not change its reported verdict.
  24: 
  25: The other main gates match the stated margins: A retention at both post-B and final, B acquisition against its own post-R1 baseline, effective-write pressure, R correction, and new-game comparisons against off and shuffled ([gates, lines 35â€“53](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-gates-2026-11-08.md:35>); [retention judge, lines 85â€“123](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-metrics-v1.mjs:85>)).
  26: 
  27: ## 3. Source-supported redesign options
  28: 
  29: - **Calibrate existing KC/APL dynamics:** Honegger et al. report sparse odor-evoked KC responses, and Lin et al. show APL feedback helps sparsify and decorrelate odor responses ([Honegger et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3180869/); [Lin et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4000970/)). This supports sparse coding as a biological design target, **not** a 5â€“10% target for this symbolic chess input or visual gamma-d KCs. A fixed, predeclared adjustment to modeled APL/KC dynamics could leave learning confined to the registered mask, but an outcome-tuned threshold or top-k filter would be an unsupported extra computation and would need a new registration.
  30: - **Subtract a resting activity vector:** this is not directly supported by the cited rule. In Gkanias et al., \(w_\text{rest}=1\) is the **resting synaptic weight**, not a resting firing-rate baseline ([Gkanias et al., equations 18â€“20](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611)). `ReversalMemory.observe(rest)` subtracts a neuronal-rate vector; revision 2 expressly uses zero and rejects counterfactual cue/DAN subtraction ([revision 2, lines 7â€“15](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-revision-2-2026-11-07.md:7>)). A blank-trial baseline would therefore change the registered rule and should not be slipped in as a parameter tweak.
  31: - **Restrict to visual gamma-d KCs:** Vogt et al. support a visual role for gamma-d KCs and visual responses in some of them ([Vogt et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4884080/)). Anatomical restriction is not extra-circuit arithmetic if fixed before training, but it only helps if the current encoder actually drives this visual pathway and the selected KCs have the relevant MBON05/PAM07 connections. It narrows the claim; it does not itself fix sparse coding or score sensitivity.
  32: 
  33: None of these sources validates a specific numerical correction for the present model. Choose and justify a mechanism before a new confirmatory design; do not tune against this dry-run outcome and then call the same run confirmatory.
  34: 
  35: ## 4. Audit, resources, scope, and limits
  36: 
  37: The auditor recomputes score metrics, update arithmetic, gates, and bootstrap without importing producer metrics, judge, or job modules ([audit module, lines 1â€“19](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-audit-v1.mjs:1>)). It does reuse packet code for tape construction and certificate verification, so it is not fully independent of packet semantics. Its graph replay is a useful reproducibility sampleâ€”four-event phase prefixes extended to both pulse kinds, plus two test-board rankingsâ€”not a replay of every teaching event ([audit runner, lines 40â€“43 and 52â€“99](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/audit-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:40>)). Static audit recomputes every receiptâ€™s update from recorded rates, but only sampled events establish those rates against a fresh graph pass. At inspection, there was no `audit.json`, so the dry-run audit verdict remains unknown. The requested unit tests finished **132/132 passing**; that validates the tested fixtures, not the missing production audit.
  38: 
  39: Resume truncates unacknowledged JSONL and restores the acknowledged snapshot; the suite includes a passing exactly-once interruption test. Writes use temporary files plus rename, worker and runner guards check RSS/free memory, and worker failure stops new jobs without silent retry ([job, lines 52â€“65](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:52>); [runner, lines 70â€“104](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/run-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:70>)). The file contract requires transitive pins, but the pin checker validates only hashes that are listed; it does not prove the list is complete ([formats, line 24](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-file-formats-2026-11-08.md:24>); [pin check, lines 865â€“880](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-audit-v1.mjs:865>)).
  40: 
  41: The declared KC-key-store and second-branch geometry cuts are acceptable if claims stay limited to the registered neural comparison and midpoint geometry. The off-arm ranking reuse was correctly removed. **Another planned cut was not implemented:** B-end still ranks testA/testB in both job code and file contract ([job, lines 11â€“16](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/src/neural-learning/fly-connections-job-v1.mjs:11>); [formats, lines 51â€“61](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-file-formats-2026-11-08.md:51>)). Recompute the full-run pass and wall budget with those rankings, or remove them consistently before pinning. The runner also explicitly requires `--cpu-duty 1`; treat that as full-utilization execution, not a pacing control ([runner, lines 22â€“24](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.codebase/scripts/neural-learning/run-fly-connections-memory-v1.mjs:22>)).
  42: 
  43: The dry run is useful Volume 2 development evidence that this implementation engages plasticity but did not acquire or transfer the criterion on its small sample. It is not confirmatory evidence. It does not prove a Volume 4 limit. A limit claim needs a bound over the declared state/input/training domain on the **change in pairwise score differences**, e.g.  
  44: \(\sup_{t,i,j}|(s_i-s_j)_t-(s_i-s_j)_0|\),  
  45: and a comparison to the smallest initial ranking margin. The observed nearly common shift on these boards is only a finite empirical measurement, not such a bound.
  46: 
  47: **STOP FOR RESEARCHER DECISION (choose between a new, source-bounded visual-KC/readout redesign and spending the full-run budget on the negative result for this exact model).**
   1: # Fly connections memory v1, design revision 2
   2: 
   3: 2026-11-07. **Draft, needs strict review. No build or launch.** Volume 2 chapters 3 and 5, P1-P3 only. Read this with the [preserved first draft](fly-connections-memory-v1-proposal-2026-11-07.md) and [SOURCE NO-GO review](../../../research/reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/design-review-r1-retry.md). This file explicitly replaces the corresponding proposed definitions below; no historical design, completed gate or evidence is edited. The full goal is unchanged.
   4: 
   5: ## 1. Remove the rejected teaching computation
   6: 
   7: There is **no cue-only/cue-plus-DAN subtraction, counterfactual neural trial, retained earlier-cue eligibility, teacher-enabled learning gate, or omission-specific D=0 branch** in the primary arm. This removes the need for a biological mechanism that distinguishes dopamine by its cause.
   8: 
   9: Use the cited Gkanias gamma4 discharging branch's **PAM07** population, not PAM08. The publisher's neuron table names MB312B / PAM-gamma4<gamma1gamma2 / PAM-07; the pinned ontology's `FBbt:00111019` distinguishes this from PAM08 (`FBbt:00111020`). Both identities, all retained roots and receiving-side gamma-KC-to-MBON05 masks must be published and independently verified before graph use. Do not rename all gamma4 DANs PAM07 or claim PAM08 equivalence. No sign overlay or population chosen from chess scores.
  10: 
  11: For each training presentation, run the cue with the scheduled experimental teaching current (positive +0.75 per mapped PAM07 cell, or zero current). From its **single actual endpoint** use `k_e=h[KC_e]/1.5` and `D_group=-mean(h[PAM07_group])/1.5`. No baseline subtraction is used: the source's nonnegative activity variables are represented by the actual nonnegative model rates, with one declared scale. Apply the existing theoretical branch `g_next=clip(g+0.5*D_group*(k_e+g-1),0.2,1.8)` once per presentation. Preserve the original Euler bound `0.5*(-D_group)<=1`; if violated, fail before mutation rather than adapt the step to the example. The original gain setter preserves the released fast sign. The zero-reference array, if the existing class is reused, is identically zero and carries no information from another experience.
  12: 
  13: This is a simultaneous, quasi-steady computational adaptation of a published theoretical rule. It is not a replication of the full Incentive Circuit, measured receptor kinetics, dopamine release concentration or biological time. It introduces no memory of an alternate neural trajectory. Transient rates are actual circuit outputs; the positive experimental current is delivered to identified neurons. Only registered synaptic gains persist. The decoder is the fixed source-motivated negative MBON05 rate; behavior, fast transmitter sign and teaching polarity remain distinct. Preserve and disclose both hemispheres' graph-sign provenance.
  14: 
  15: **Endogenous dopamine is retained.** Every scheduled presentation in informative, absent, shuffled and mechanism-control arms invokes the local rule using that arm's actual neural endpoint, whether or not an external pulse was supplied. Therefore absent means **no external teaching**, not no synaptic change. The learning-off arm is the exact no-update floor. This directly addresses the v1 control mistake through the correct estimand: informative must improve from its own naive state and beat both absent and learning-off; no claim that sensory DAN activity vanished. If sensory activity saturates the teaching pathway or makes all arms equivalent, that is a failed mechanism result; do not subtract it away afterward.
  16: 
  17: No negative current, negative dopamine concentration, arbitrary punishment channel or positive dopaminergic factor is introduced. Positive task outcomes evoke the source-matched rewarding-population current. Other known outcomes have no externally delivered pulse, but remain actual modeled sensory/neuronal experiences. Unknown verification produces no scheduled teaching experience; log it separately. In a balanced tape, the shuffled arm receives the same pulse count, presented through the same mapped neurons at permuted known events. Independent audit records measured DAN responses, not just requested pulses.
  18: 
  19: ## 2. Specify the APL recurrence and strength control
  20: 
  21: Keep the first draft's roots, existing retained contact masks, per-contact division of each KC-to-APL aggregate magnitude, tree kernel, length scale and fixed signs. For each APL output contact y and neural step t:
  22: 
  23: `u_y[t] = model.drive[APL] + sum(nonKC i -> APL, signedMagnitude_i * h_i[t]) + sum(KC contacts x, magnitude_edge(x)/count_edge(x) * h_KC(x)[t] * K(x,y))`.
  24: 
  25: `a_y[t+1]=(1-alpha_APL)*a_y[t]+alpha_APL*relu_tanh(u_y[t], model.saturation)`.
  26: 
  27: Here `alpha_APL` and `relu_tanh` are exactly the unchanged simulator's leak and activation. Initialize every a_y[0]=0 for every candidate. The original whole-cell APL state remains for its non-KC outputs. In the recurrence for a KC at step t, subtract each original `signedMagnitude(APL->KC)*h_APL[t]` contribution exactly once and insert that signed magnitude times the mean a_y[t] on that ordered pair's output contacts. Local state at t+1 never feeds the KC's step t. No double count, extra edge or persistent local field.
  28: 
  29: An explicit **global kernel mode K=1**, not numeric Infinity passed into the existing prototype, must reproduce the ordinary scalar APL path on independent small fixtures, including non-KC input and external drive. Both use the same arrays/rounding and original initialization where exact equality is possible; otherwise freeze a justified numerical tolerance before graph use. Nonfinite inputs and missing pair contacts fail.
  30: 
  31: Add **informative / strength-matched-nonlocal-APL**. It computes the same local field from its actual neural state, then substitutes one uniform value for all APL-to-KC outputs: `U[t]=sum_e(magnitude_e*mean_y(a_y[t]))/sum_e(magnitude_e)`, separately per APL cell. Thus, for that exact presynaptic state, total outgoing inhibitory current equals its local field's total, but the spatial distribution is removed. Across diverging arms, total trajectories need not be identical; disclose that conditional matching. This is an engineered causal control, not source physiology. Original-global-APL remains a separate diagnostic ablation. Computational costs of the local-field and strength-matched paths are matched.
  32: 
  33: Local APL is **a candidate stabiliser**, not a demonstrated stabiliser or a necessarily stronger decorrelator. Separate three conclusions: P1-P3 joint success; locality's causal contribution relative to strength-matched nonlocal output; and biological fidelity. A joint memory pass does not require falsely attributing its cause to APL if other conditions pass too. No added APL-superiority gate redefines the researcher's P1-P3 requirement.
  34: 
  35: Geometry alternatives remain finite stress cases, never uncertainty bounds or proof of exact attachment. Define distinct alternative branches by maximal skeleton chains whose internal vertices have degree two; for the alternate projection choose the closest segment on a different chain within 100 nm of the best residual, with stable segment-ID ties. Report all cases, including those with no alternate. The complete canonical sample's known APL-side substitution is another finite stress condition. Do not infer unseen branch correctness from passing them. Freeze the primary midpoint condition and report geometry dependence; no winner substitution.
  36: 
  37: ## 3. Make the experience budget explicit and the seeds real
  38: 
  39: Replace the first draft's all-actions x four-epochs tape. Before neural data, the teacher independently verifies the complete legal action set on each training board, selects two safe and two unsafe non-king capture experiences in a fixed SHA-256 order (cycling if only one of a class exists), and schedules these **four training events per board, one pass**. This is a disclosed enriched teaching curriculum. It is fixed from training-only certificate results, not neural scores. It supplies both kinds of verified experience and does not filter the learner's ranked move list: **every ranking before every event still contains every legal move**, including all quiet and king actions. The fly sees no marker identifying the teacher-selected subset. It is a fixed-experience assay, not self-chosen exploration or a test of arbitrary natural game distributions.
  40: 
  41: For each seed, independently permute the 32 A boards, the 32 B boards and the four scheduled actions within a board; preserve the A-before-B phase order. All arms in that seed consume the **same realized board/action tape**. All use the same naive graph parameters; the seed genuinely varies experience order, rather than unused input/head arrays. Save the permutation/PRNG identity, seeds and whole tape hash before propagation. No best-order selection. Shuffled reinforcement is a separate seeded permutation within the corresponding complete A/B/R phase and retains exact pulse counts.
  42: 
  43: One event: rank all legal candidates from the unchanged snapshot; save the complete ranking and state fingerprint; verify the selected action's exact certificate; replay that selected cue with the scheduled external current; read the actual endpoint; compute one local update; publish the pending receipt and next checkpoint before acknowledgment. In a no-current event this last replay still occurs with zero experimental current. All arms have the same numbers of observation/replay passes. Evaluation never advances learning or transient/persistent teaching clocks.
  44: 
  45: Let L(b) be the complete legal count of board b. The training pass count per arm/seed is exactly `sum_b 4*(L(b)+1)`, plus registered probes, correction phase and restore/audit checks. If candidate-order invariance reruns every ranking, count its second L(b) too; it is not a free check. Freeze all probes and their counts in registration. Source geometry compilation has a separate registered offline budget and no neural learning.
  46: 
  47: The earlier 24/72-hour durations are **not verified runtime estimates**. A detached dry run pairs informative teaching with controls and provides bounded actual measurements of the implemented path. Before full launch, derive exact pass counts from the frozen tape and combine them with its measured pass/resource costs and declared headroom; either register a justified wall/pass budget or reject the workload. Do not run a separate untrained graph benchmark when the informative dry run can supply that measurement. No silent extension or unchanged-budget retry after a guard failure.
  48: 
  49: ## 4. Acquisition, retention and pressure
  50: 
  51: Preserve the researcher's **0.70** margin-retention bar, the after-A and after-B checkpoints, all-seed requirement, finite fixed synapse mask and every-overwrite report. Replace the first draft's margin by the actual choice margin `m_b=max(score of safe non-king captures)-max(score of all other legal actions)`. This measures choosing any criterion-successful move. The all-legal s/c measure still handles top ties. Positive naive preference is not counted as newly acquired memory without improvement.
  52: 
  53: At T_A, an A board is acquired only if its all-legal success probability >=0.75, its choice margin > the frozen score tie tolerance, and its margin exceeds its own T0 margin by >1e-6. Require at least 24/32 A boards acquired; record every unacquired board as a failure, not an exclusion from the report. The acquired set is determined on **training evidence only**, never test evidence.
  54: 
  55: After B, require every acquired A board to remain successful (s/c >=0.75) and retain `(m_AB-m_0)/(m_A-m_0)>=0.70`. Also retain the full original group-level learned-margin ratio >=0.70 over all 32 A boards with a strictly positive denominator, so redefining an acquired subset cannot hide group loss. Publish all per-board ratios, raw margins, final choices and all lost memories. B acquisition requires >=24/32 B boards meeting the same success/absolute-margin/improvement criteria relative to **T_B0, immediately after R acquisition and before B learning**. Primary A/B success must improve from its own corresponding prelearning checkpoint and beat absent, learning-off and shuffled; compute game-clustered bootstrap bounds, report each seed separately. T_A remains A's post-acquisition reference; R presentations must not be hidden when defining B's baseline.
  56: 
  57: An overwrite record contains the edge's previous and new gain **and the actually applied Float32 magnitude bits/values**. Repeated A-to-B writes count toward the operational pressure threshold only if they change effective bits and the effective magnitude by at least 1% of that edge's naive magnitude. Require such B writes on >=25% of A-written edges, and >=25% of the complete mask to have effective changes of that size by T_AB. Report all smaller/tail changes separately. These are numerical stress thresholds, not an estimate of biological bits/maximum associations. Distinguish shared-state competition, saturation and actual behavioral memory loss. A touched-edge total alone cannot establish meaningful pressure.
  58: 
  59: Use the same memory budget at every load; no association slots, edge allocation by task ID or growing learned storage. Source/readout/representation/weights remain otherwise fixed. Report growth of losses and effective writes at loads 8,16,32 in each group. Interpret a pass only for this measured pressure range; do not claim unlimited capacity or zero overwriting.
  60: 
  61: ## 5. Test correction of an acquired preference honestly
  62: 
  63: Add four **explicit experimental reward-contingency probes R**, with source games/boards disjoint from A, B and final-test sets. This is an association/reversal mechanism prerequisite inside the same registered campaign, not a claim that a true chess safety certificate changed. Every R action is legal and fully ranked against all legal moves. Before neural observation choose two distinct target legal actions by a fixed hash order. Their targets are opaque to the fly; only experimental stimulation conveys the outcome.
  64: 
  65: R phase 1, after learning A: four cycles of both target actions per R board, with a positive teaching pulse for the first target and no external pulse for the other. Require each R board's first target to have s/c >=0.75 and positive choice margin against all other legal actions, improved from its pre-R checkpoint. At T_AB, before reversal, require that acquired first target still meets this successful-preference condition; losing it during B is not successful correction. R phase 2, after B and before final freezing: exchange these **declared artificial reward contingencies**, with the same four-cycle/two-action exposure. Require each board's second target to achieve s/c >=0.75 and a positive margin, and the formerly acquired target to cease being the sole preferred action. A mere margin increase that leaves the old choice dominant fails correction.
  66: 
  67: An R certificate binds full board/history, UCI action, experimental-contingency criterion version, planned phase and intended delivered current; the independent auditor checks legal replay and exact predeclared contingent stimulation. It never calls these records safe/unsafe chess consequences. Their neural response/update receipts use the same source-matched pathway and local rule as A/B. All controls share the R experience tape and count/timing; shuffled reinforcement preserves each R phase's pulse count. No label/phase ID is an input to the fly or a selector for different synapses.
  68: 
  69: After R acquisition and after R reversal, re-test A and B. The acquired A retention and B success gates must remain satisfied; correction that erases unrelated acquired A memories fails the joint result. No artificial R success substitutes for chess A/B acquisition or P3. If R cannot be justified as a source-bounded prerequisite within this goal, reject this definition before build rather than mislabel the reversed outcomes.
  70: 
  71: ## 6. Comparisons, P3 and reporting
  72: 
  73: Primary and five neural comparisons: informative-local; absent-local; learning-off-local; shuffled-local; informative-strength-matched-nonlocal; informative-original-global. Informative, absent and shuffled use their actual local DAN activity on every scheduled cue, including endogenous activity. Learning-off presents the same informative neural inputs but holds the complete persistent memory fixed. No absent arm is called a no-update floor.
  74: 
  75: Raw Eyes and fixed random-feature external stores receive the identical scheduled training-board/action tape and its positive/zero **delivered external stimulation code**, not additional safety proofs, unseen labels, or extra candidate exposures. They may decode pulse/no-pulse because they are engineered comparisons. They do not acquire biological status, and are empirical comparisons, not guaranteed ceilings. A separate disclosed v8-style store uses the same exposure budget. Pin its exact key/normalization/capacity/update/tie rule and random projection before launch; use only training data to set any scaler. The principal fly has none of these stores, labels or fitted feature transforms.
  76: 
  77: Report uniform random-legal, uniform random-over-all-captures (including legal king captures) and uniform random-over-non-king-captures. For a capture-empty board, report that policy's exact fallback (uniform all-legal) instead of omitting the board. The benchmarks are **diagnostics**, not added completion gates or reasons to modify the task after data.
  78: 
  79: P3 retains the original handoff's fresh-game, all-legal s/c comparison with learning-off and shuffled, with positive game-clustered 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 10,000 paired resamples in every seed. Also require improvement from the primary's own naive checkpoint; use paired within-game change, not a damaged control alone. Report A/B test strata separately and jointly with frozen group/sample membership. P1 biological/source applicability and P2 gates remain necessary; P3 never repairs their failure. Source data and family/board/input-collision exclusions must be frozen before final neural evaluation; do not re-use an observed old test result as sealed confirmation.
  80: 
  81: Full confirmation is one new immutable registered run containing all seeds, arms, memory phases, checkpoint/restore checks and final evaluations. Development is not confirmation. Exact masks, transitive code/input/registration hashes, game IDs, seeds, permutations, task/certificate/decoder identities, budgets, pending-update recovery and independent auditor must be registered before launch. Report P1-P3 line by line with limitations first. Source physiology, computational approximations and experimental evidence remain separate.
  82: 
  83: ## 7. Remaining build/launch boundaries
  84: 
  85: The first review's integration, certificate, collision, independent replay, resource and publication checks still apply. Standard board/certificate helpers may not silently substitute their old failure-triggered pulse or unknown-king behavior for this declared task. No frozen source is edited to retrofit a pass. The APL local component remains isolated, sparse and bounded. All new implementation uses Haiku xhigh, small tasks, at most four; every prompt starts with the required repository ponytail instruction. Main agent tests/reviews, strict Codex code review, detached informative dry run plus independent audit, then a frozen full run plus independent audit.
  86: 
  87: No build follows until strict review explicitly allows this revised source/design. The key question is whether actual local PAM07 activity with the disclosed published negative DPR branch is a sufficient biological justification for P1, not whether extra-circuit arithmetic can be renamed. If a necessary source fact still cannot be resolved, record it and use the researcher's honest-stop condition. Do not weaken P1 to engineered-memory success.
  88: 
  89: Static evidence before review: the pinned-name annotation table contains 18 PAM07 DAN roots (10 right, 8 left), all present in the actual NPZ root array. [Root crosswalk](../../../research/reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/source-evidence/pam07-static-root-check-r2.json). This establishes retained identities, not receptor dynamics, full mask authentication or successful propagation. Exact graph/annotation/ontology hashes, receiving-side masks and full independent source applicability remain prelaunch checks.
   1: # Fly connections memory v1: run protocol and gates (2026-11-08)
   2: 
   3: **Draft for strict code-and-protocol review. Written before any dry-run or full-run data.** Volume 2, chapters 3 (To Learn) and 5 (Evidence for Learning). Properties P1, P2, P3 of the [memory handoff](../research-goals/memory-handoff-2026-11-07.md). Design: [revision 2](fly-connections-memory-v1-revision-2-2026-11-07.md) ([BUILD ALLOWED](../../../research/reports/fly-connections-memory-v1-2026-11-07/design-review-r2.md)). This file fixes the numbers revision 2 left open. Where it conflicts with revision 2, revision 2 wins unless a line below says "changes revision 2" and gives the reason.
   4: 
   5: ## 0. Clues and help, first
   6: 
   7: 1. **Board selection.** Every A, B and test board is chosen to contain at least one safe and one unsafe non-king capture, all with complete certificates. The fly is told nothing about this, and it ranks **all legal moves**, but a safe answer always exists on the board. Uniform-random baselines (all legal, all captures, non-king captures) are reported beside every score to show how much this selection helps.
   8: 2. **Teacher-chosen practice.** On each training board the teacher picks two safe and two unsafe captures to replay. The fly's own ranking is recorded before each event but does not choose the practised move. This is a fixed curriculum, not exploration.
   9: 3. **Positive valence only.** A verified safe capture gets a +0.75 current into the 18 PAM07 cells. Unsafe captures get no external current. Unknown certificates produce no event.
  10: 4. **Success criterion.** Success is choosing a safe non-king capture. Quiet and king moves are legal choices that count as failures.
  11: 5. **Shared data.** All three seeds use the same boards; only the order of experience differs. Seeds are not independent replications.
  12: 6. **Status.** First data for this design. The full run is confirmatory for this design only if no gate below changes after the dry run; any change is recorded as a pivot and named in the report.
  13: 
  14: ## 1. Data
  15: 
  16: | Group | Full run | Dry run | Source |
  17: |---|---|---|---|
  18: | A (learn first) | 32 boards | 8 | train-bucket games 901-1200 (full) / 601-900 (dry) |
  19: | R (contingency probes) | 4 | 2 | same pool as A, disjoint games |
  20: | B (learn second) | 32 | 8 | same pool, disjoint games |
  21: | testA, testB | 60 + 60 | 10 + 10 | **development bucket**, first 300 games of the fixed sample order, fetched fresh and never used by any experiment (full) / train games 301-600 (dry) |
  22: 
  23: One board per game: the first eligible board per game in preserved source order. Group membership is fixed by a hash of the game ID before any neural pass. Test boards whose board key (FEN without move counters) or rendered Eyes input equals any training board's are excluded and listed. The dry run never reads development-bucket games.
  24: 
  25: ## 2. Arms (all receive the identical experience tape in each seed)
  26: 
  27: Neural, local APL unless named: **informative** (primary), **absent** (no external current), **off** (all persistent memory held fixed), **shuffled** (same pulse count, permuted within each phase), **informative-matched** (strength-matched nonlocal APL), **informative-global** (original scalar APL). Engineered comparisons, graph-free: **raw-pc** and **random-pc** nearest-episode stores (v5 transforms, capacity 4096, k 3) fed the same tape with the delivered pulse code, each also with the shuffled code. Uniform-random baselines.
  28: 
  29: Seeds (experience order): full 7301, 7302, 7303; dry 7300. Naive model seed 47 for every arm. Bootstrap seed 20261109, 10,000 paired resamples over games, lower quantile 0.025.
  30: 
  31: ## 3. Checkpoints
  32: 
  33: T0 (naive), A8, A16, T_A (A end), T_B0 (after R1, before B), B8, B16, T_AB (B end), final (after R2). Test boards are ranked at T0, T_AB and final. Training boards are ranked at every checkpoint where revision 2 needs them. Every arm, the off arm included, ranks at every checkpoint, so pass counts are equal (changed after code review r1, before data; the earlier off-arm reuse of T0 rankings is withdrawn).
  34: 
  35: ## 4. Gates (every one must pass in every seed)
  36: 
  37: **P1, fly connections** (informative arm):
  38: 1. The only persistent state that changes is the ReversalMemory gain on the registered gamma-KC-to-MBON05 mask edges. Every other parameter, magnitude outside the mask, sign and topology keeps its T0 fingerprint at every checkpoint.
  39: 2. Every update is recomputed by the auditor from its receipt (actual KC rates, actual PAM07 rates, the Gkanias rule with eta 0.5, gain bounds 0.2-1.8, activity scale 1.5) and matches bit for bit.
  40: 3. The teaching pathway works: over all A and B events, the mean measured D on pulse events is lower (more depressing) than on no-pulse events by more than 1e-6, in this arm.
  41: 4. No engineered store, label, task ID or selected-action list enters the primary arm's scores (code review and auditor static check).
  42: 
  43: **P2, lasts while learning** (informative arm):
  44: 1. `judgeRetention` passes with minAcquired 24 (dry 6), retention 0.70, epsilon 1e-6: at least 24 of 32 A boards acquired at T_A; every acquired A board still successful (s/c >= 0.75) with learned-margin ratio >= 0.70 at T_AB **and** at final; the all-32-board group ratio >= 0.70 at both; at least 24 of 32 B boards acquired at T_AB against T_B0 and still successful at final.
  45: 2. A and B learning is caused by teaching: per-board success over A and B at T_AB beats absent, off and shuffled, each with a game-clustered lower bound above 0.
  46: 2b. A and B success improves from each group's own baseline (added after code review r1, before data): per-board success of A at T_AB beats A at T0, and per-board success of B at T_AB beats B at T_B0, each paired by game with a lower bound above 0.
  47: 3. Pressure was real: `summarizePressure` passes (B writes change at least 25% of A-written edges by at least 1% of naive magnitude, and at least 25% of the mask changes by at least 1% by T_AB). If this fails, P2 is untested, which counts as not passed.
  48: 4. Correction works: on every R board, the phase-1 target is acquired after R1 (s/c >= 0.75, margin above the tie tolerance, improved from before R1) and still holds at T_AB; after R2 the phase-2 target has s/c >= 0.75 and a positive margin, and the phase-1 target is no longer the sole top choice.
  49: 5. Reported, not gated: capacity (mask size), every effective overwrite with old and new Float32 values, losses and effective writes at loads 8, 16 and 32 per group.
  50: 
  51: **P3, new positions, no clues** (informative arm, test boards, all legal moves, final checkpoint):
  52: 1. Per-board success beats off and beats shuffled, each with a game-clustered lower bound above 0.
  53: 2. Per-board success improves from its own T0, paired by game, lower bound above 0.
  54: 
  55: **Fair and checked:**
  56: 1. Identical tape in all arms of a seed (tape SHA-256 in every job).
  57: 2. Peak RSS <= 2048 MiB in every process; free memory >= 256 MiB at every guard check. A guard failure fails the run; no silent retry.
  58: 3. Independent audit PASS, with all pins matching.
  59: 
  60: **Done** = all of the above in every seed of the one registered full run.
  61: 
  62: ## 4b. Scope changes from revision 2 (declared before data, code review r1)
  63: 
  64: 1. **No v8-style KC-key store.** Revision 2 section 6 listed it as a comparison. It is dropped: goal condition 5 names only the raw-input and random-feature arms, and in v8 both of those beat the KC key with the same store. The report may not say how a fly-key engineered store compares here.
  65: 2. **No second-branch APL geometry.** Only the primary midpoint geometry is run. Claims about local APL are limited to that geometry, and the run says nothing about dependence on attachment uncertainty.
  66: 3. **Test boards are task-selected.** P3's "new positions" means boards from unseen games that contain a safe and an unsafe non-king capture, not an unrestricted sample of unseen positions.
  67: 4. **No APL attribution arms in the full run** (declared after the dry run's cost measurement, before any full-run data). The strength-matched and global APL arms run in the dry run only. Measured cost was about 0.73 CPU-s per pass, so six arms would need about 160 CPU-hours. They are not gates (section 2 and revision 2 section 2: locality's causal contribution is a separate question). The full run therefore cannot say whether local APL inhibition caused any result.
  68: 
  69: ## 5. What a pass would and would not show
  70: 
  71: Would show: lasting, teaching-dependent changes in modelled fly synapses, driven through identified dopamine neurons by a published rule, that keep earlier lessons under measured overwrite pressure and improve all-legal choices on boards from unseen games. Would not show: biological fidelity (timescales, receptor kinetics, measured release), reasoning or lessons (P4, Volume 3), chess skill beyond safe-capture choice, independent replication (seeds share data), or that local APL caused the result (the matched and global arms answer that separately and are not gates).
  72: 
  73: ## 6. Budget
  74: 
  75: Exact pass counts come from the frozen tapes (revision 2 section 3) plus the checkpoint rankings above. The dry run measures seconds per pass and peak RSS for each arm. Before the full launch, the registration states: passes per job, workers W = min(7, floor((free MiB - 1024) / peak job RSS)), and a wall guard of 1.5 x (measured seconds per pass x passes per job). A run that exceeds it fails.

