research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:159:## 18. 2026-11-09: diagnostic before any redesign: KC threshold and sparseness
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:161:- **Why this diagnostic is allowed:** it answers one named, gate-relevant question that blocks the feedback experiment (can the gamma-KC code separate moves on one board at a sourced sparseness?). Read-only, dev boards (dry-run packet), no learning, 3 boards x 6 thresholds. Next feedback-enabled step: a revision-3 proposal with the chosen threshold, reviewed, then a new dry run.
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:166:- **Diagnostic result 1 (KC threshold only; 3 dev A boards, 106 passes per threshold, no learning):** `runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/kc-sparseness-diagnostic-v1/`. Fraction of mapped gamma KCs active: theta 0: 100%; 0.005: 100%; 0.01 (KC bias 0): 99.9%; 0.012: 98.9%; 0.015: 88.2%; 0.02: 0.65%. Move-specific cells (active for some but not all moves of a board): at most 3.1% of the mask (theta 0.015). Median within-board KC difference (L2 over sum of norms) rises only from 0.006 to 0.029. Reading: even with no KC drive, KCs stay active because the rest of the network is driven by the same 0.01 bias; a KC threshold alone gives an all-or-none switch, not a sparse move-specific code. Next diagnostic (same question): zero tonic drive in every neuron (scope all), so only the eyes' stimulation drives activity.
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:167:- **Diagnostic result 2 (zero or reduced tonic drive in every neuron; same 3 boards):** `runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/kc-sparseness-diagnostic-v1-scope-all/`. Bias 0.005: 100% of gamma KCs active, within-board difference 0.008. **Bias 0 (no tonic drive): 14.4% active (about 360 of 2,096 per board), 5.5% of the mask move-specific, within-board difference 0.118 (about 20 times the dry run's 0.006), MBON05 score spread 3e-5 to 9e-5.** Bias -0.001: 1.6% active and MBON05 silent (score spread 0). Reading: the dense code is caused by the trainer's uniform 0.01 drive, an engineering initialization with no biological claim; with no tonic drive the eye stimulus alone gives a sparse code in the range sources describe as sparse (Honegger: under 20%; Lin: 5-10%, unverified). Activity is then very low (mean active rate 0.0005), so whether the rule can still change a ranking is the next question (learning probe, informative vs off, 2 dev boards).
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:169:## 19. 2026-11-09 00:41: dry-run audit: AUDIT FAIL on pins only
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:172:- **Learning probe (zero drive, informative arm, dev board Q04sIPQ5:13, its 4 teacher experiences repeated 4 times = 16 events, 8 pulses):** `runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/zero-drive-learning-probe-v1.log`. The practised safe move's score moved from -6.732e-5 to -6.715e-5 (1.7e-7) and stayed ranked 32nd; margin unchanged to 5 digits. D with pulse -0.461, without -0.00005 (endogenous dopamine nearly gone at zero drive). Mean k over active KCs 0.00044 (max 0.0148). Reading: with zero drive the code is sparse, but rates are so low that the source rule's k-proportional change is negligible. The probe was stopped after board 2 began (PID 12204, by me): the off arm adds nothing to this question. Next: is the stimulus too weak? Sweep the eye-current gain (10, 30, 100) at zero drive and measure KC rates, sparseness and within-board separation.
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:175:## 20. 2026-11-09: STOP under the goal's stop clause
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:179:## 21. 2026-11-09: continuing after the stop hook: calibration probe before any revision 3
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:181:The goal's hook ruled that the stop clause is not met; the goal authorizes design and review, so I proceed with the calibration route myself and will send it to strict review before any build. **Probe question (gate-relevant, development boards only, no registration):** with zero tonic drive and the rule's KC term read on an activity-derived scale (k = min(1, rate / 0.022); 0.022 is the largest gamma-KC rate seen in the zero-drive probe, chosen from activity alone, not from chess outcomes), and the DAN factor unchanged (scale 1.5), does teaching change a within-board ranking? Script `probe-zero-drive-learning-v2.mjs` (copy of v1 plus a probe-only patch of `observe`; informative arm only).
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:182:- **Probe v2 result (board Q04sIPQ5:13, 16 events):** scores now move 18% (practised safe move -6.732e-5 to -5.533e-5), but the practised no-pulse move moves almost as much (-7.078e-5 to -5.867e-5); rank stays 32; margin -1.684e-5, -1.535, -1.447, -1.395, -1.365 (e-5) with shrinking steps (0.149, 0.088, 0.052, 0.030), so it levels off below 0. Reading: moves on one board share most of their active KCs, so depression lifts all moves; a KC scale alone does not give within-board discrimination. Probe stopped by me after board 1 (the question was answered).
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:184:## 22. 2026-11-09: oracle-gains construction (Volume 2 fix or Volume 4 limit?)
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:189:## 23. 2026-11-09: remaining routes all need the researcher
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:193:## 24. 2026-11-09: multi-rival oracle bound (question recorded before running)
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:195:After the hook's third ruling: the decisive remaining question is whether ANY setting of the 2,096 mask gains in [0.2, 1.8] makes a safe capture the top choice over all legal moves. Method: dump each legal move's per-edge presynaptic KC rates (`probe-oracle-lp-v1.mjs`, Haiku-built), fit a linear score model (sensitivity from an all-gains-0.9 ranking), solve max-min over all rivals through its dual (`python actions/solve_oracle_lp_v1.py`, mine; self-checked: 0.6 on a solvable case, -1.0 certified on an impossible one), then verify the best gains exactly on the graph. A positive exact margin is an existence witness (the rule, not the synapses, is the limit). A negative dual bound is a certificate **for the linear model only**; its error is measured by comparing predicted and exact margins. Development boards Q04sIPQ5:13, SjmcbcGu:16, 5XRGyGSj:13; bias 0 and 0.01.
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:209:## 25. 2026-11-09: KC representation capacity (question recorded before running)
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:225:## 26. 2026-11-09: widened-mask feasibility screen (question recorded before running)
research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:227:Sources for widening: Gkanias et al. 2022 Table 1 (verbatim via eLife page: 12 neurons, 8 DAN-to-MBON plastic connections, roles sat/sav/rat/rav/mat/mav, dat/dav/cat/cav/fat/fav; PAM-gamma4<gamma1gamma2 named reward-encoding verbatim). Connectome types from the pinned FBbt ontology synonyms: MBON11 (gamma1pedc), MBON05 (gamma4>gamma1gamma2), MBON12 (gamma2alpha'1), MBON01 (gamma5beta'2a), MBON02 (beta2beta'2a), MBON15 (alpha'1; MBON31 also matched a prefix, to resolve), PAM07, PAM02 (beta'2a), PAM03 (beta2beta'2a), PPL101 (gamma1pedc), PPL103 (gamma2alpha'1). **Not verified verbatim:** PAM-beta'2a as reward-encoding (from a page summary). Screen (`python actions/incentive_mask_screen_v1.py`, mine): with gains in [0.2, 1.8] on every KC input to the six MBON types and the source valence readout (attraction minus avoidance), can a safe capture be made top? KC-drive proxy only (no dynamics, non-KC inputs ignored): a necessary-capacity screen, not an exact test.
