## Section 6

1. **Not as a “full Gkanias incentive circuit.”** The paper describes the circuit as having MBON-to-DAN feedback and MBON-to-MBON connections, alongside KC-to-MBON plasticity; revision 4 expressly leaves out the source feedback weights and biases (proposal lines 28–30; [Gkanias et al., circuit and Table 1](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611#results)). A narrower claim—a connectome model applying a published plasticity rule to selected fly-mapped synapses—could be defensible after the rule and mappings are corrected. Do not call that the full circuit.

2. **Positive-only teaching is a defensible task restriction, with a limited claim.** PAM07 maps to the source’s reward discharging neuron, and the paper’s reward input also drives its charging reward neuron, the role assigned to PAM02. The source supports those roles and mappings; it does not establish that the proposed `+0.75` current to every mapped cell reproduces its reinforcement input. No punishment current is consistent with a positive-only intervention, but actual endogenous DAN activity still has to enter the update. State this as a computational teaching intervention, not a complete recreation of the source’s reward/punishment protocol. (Proposal lines 23–28; [source Table 1 and reinforcement mapping](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611#materials-and-methods).)

3. **Find and implement the source’s combination first.** The paper computes one dopamine factor per MBON from the DAN activity vector and a signed DAN-to-KC/MBON matrix, then applies that factor to each KC-to-MBON weight. Its equation is a weighted sum across DANs, not separate per-DAN-row gains multiplied together. The proposal itself leaves this combination open (line 28). More fundamentally, Table 1 lists **10**, not eight, DAN-to-MBON plastic connections (the entries at Table 1 rows 155, 157, 161, 163, 165, 167, 169, 171, 173 and 174 in the publisher text). The claimed eight-row mask and per-row gain representation need correction before implementation. [Equations 12, 18 and 19; Table 1](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611#materials-and-methods).

4. **No.** Keeping trainer drive at 0.01 avoids changing that setting, but does not make it source-derived. The stop report identifies the uniform drive and the rate-to-rule scale as unresolved model choices (lines 29–36). The paper specifies its own circuit biases, activity transformations and learning equation; it does not validate this model’s uniform drive or `rate / 1.5` mapping. Call both explicit engineering assumptions, or provide a justified mapping. ([Revision 2, lines 11–13](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/fly-connections-memory-v1-revision-2-2026-11-07.md:11>); [source model and equations](https://elifesciences.org/articles/75611#materials-and-methods).)

## Evidence items 24–26

- **Item 24 supports a bound for its linear surrogate, not an exact-network impossibility result.** The script fits per-edge score sensitivities around gains 0.9, then solves the surrogate max-min problem; the logged upper bounds for the safe moves on two zero-drive boards are negative ([script lines 3–7, 38–50](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/python%20actions/solve_oracle_lp_v1.py:3>); [journal item 24](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/research/journal/experiments/2026-11-07-fly-connections-memory.md:193>); [zero-drive log, lines 1–15](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/oracle-lp-v1.log:1>)). The script’s dual expression can give an upper bound for that linear model. But the fitted linearization’s observed 2–15% error is not a bound over the gain range. Exact replay verifies that the proposed gain settings still lose; it does not verify that every setting loses ([verification log, lines 4–6](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/oracle-lp-v1-verify.log:4>)). So “exactly verified at zero drive” and the proposal’s exact-network claim overstate the evidence.
- **The default-drive failure is supported for the tested candidate settings.** The surrogate predicts positive margins, while exact replay gives negative margins on all three boards ([oracle log, lines 19–33](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/oracle-lp-v1.log:19>); [verification log, lines 1–3](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/oracle-lp-v1-verify.log:1>)). This is good evidence that the default-drive linearization is unreliable there—not a proof that no exact-network gain setting can succeed.
- **Item 25 is a representation-capacity probe, not proof the widened circuit can learn.** It allows an oracle linear readout over all 5,177 KCs, rather than the proposed anatomical synapses and source DAN update ([script lines 1–8, 21–39](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/python%20actions/kc_capacity_v1.py:1>)). The log reports positive found margins, but the script discards the candidate weights and does not exactly verify them ([log lines 1–6](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/kc-capacity-v1.log:1>)). It suggests the recorded KC responses contain separable information under this oracle setup; it does not establish a source-supported readout or transfer.
- **Item 26 is only a proxy feasibility witness.** Its score is valence-weighted KC drive from independently adjustable gains on positive KC edges; it explicitly ignores network dynamics and non-KC inputs ([script lines 1–8, 37–62](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/python%20actions/incentive_mask_screen_v1.py:1>)). The positive margins support “this proxy can separate the moves,” not “the source-rule circuit has capacity” or “no rate calibration is needed.” The script also discards the candidate gain vector, so the witness is not preserved in the log ([screen log, lines 1–7](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/runs/fly-connections-memory-2026-11-08/incentive-mask-screen-v1.log:1>)).

## Required changes

**BUILD**

1. Correct the source transcription: use the 10 Table 1 plastic modulation rows, implement the signed DAN-to-MBON aggregation from the paper, and maintain one KC-to-MBON weight per synapse. Pin exact ontology identifiers and root crosswalks. MBON15 and MBON31 are distinct terms; the ontology identifies MBON15’s `alpha'1` synonym separately from MBON31’s `alpha'1a` ([ontology MBON15 lines 208649–208655 and MBON31 lines 119939–119944](</C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/.cache/fbbt.obo:208649>)).
2. Narrow the “full circuit” claim or model the source circuit components it omits. Keep PAM02’s reward role and any current to it explicitly labeled as a modeling intervention.
3. Correct the proposal and linked evidence claims: item 24 is a linear-surrogate bound with exact checks of selected settings, while items 25–26 are proxy/oracle capacity evidence. Record the default-drive exact losses without generalizing them to all possible gains.
4. State the 0.01 drive and rate/activity scale as engineering choices; do not present them as source-derived or as eliminating the calibration issue.

**LAUNCH**

1. Before a confirmatory run, run a new development dry run and independent audit on the corrected, pinned build. Preserve candidate vectors and exact replay results for capacity witnesses. Do not use the current proxy or zero-drive linear result as an exact-network gate.

The source gives a path to a corrected, narrower experiment, so the current proposal is not ready to build as written.

